; A pit with a trampoline in it (issue #54, #52's BouncePad). Two banks, the
; same shape and height as Gauntlet's (tops y=300, x=+-290..+-660), flank a
; 580 px pit -- far too wide to cross by hooking. What spans it is the pad
; sunk in its middle: drop in, land on the pad, get launched straight back
; up, and swing the arm to steer the fall onto whichever side you want.
;
; **The pad.** x=-100..100, top y=372, 190 px short of either bank lip, so
; it is reached by dropping, never by walking. launch_speed is 1250 rather
; than the part's default 2000: under the player's gravity (1.5 x 980) that
; is a ~530 px rise, apex around y=-160 -- well inside the camera's -450 top
; edge, so a launch is never an off-screen blind fall. A fall into the pit
; is therefore not a ring-out by itself; it is a second chance that fires
; you back into the fight. Missing the pad is.
;
; **The nests.** x=+-130..+-290, top y=100: 200 px above the banks, so the
; staff (240 reach) can climb to one from its bank, and everyone else gets
; there off the pad. Their inner edges stop 130 px off centre, clear of the
; launch column, so a straight launch never slams into a nest's underside.
; They hold two of the pickups, which is what makes the pad worth riding.
;
; Spawns sit on the banks, as on Gauntlet: nobody starts over the pit. The
; rising floor (ADR-0012) climbs to the spawns' height and takes the pad
; with it, which leaves the nests as the last high ground late in a round.
; They are reachable then only by the staff's climb or a fling from a bank,
; so a camper up there can still be reached.

; Backdrop (issue #117): evening.
